course: geography
lesson: 35
title: The Mughals, the Ming and the empires of Asia
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the years Europa Universalis 5 covers, the richest and most crowded lands on Earth were not in Europe but in Asia: India and China each had far more people than all of western Europe.
explain: In 1337 the Mongol Yuan ruled China and the Delhi Sultanate ruled much of north India; the Ming and the Mughals, the famous names, did not exist yet and arose later, just as they do in the game.
explain: Think of a school where the head teacher changes every few generations: the building stays, but the rules and the language of the office change, and so did the dynasties of China, India and Persia.
explain: These empires left wonders you can still visit: the Taj Mahal, built by an emperor as a tomb for his wife, the Forbidden City in Beijing, and most of the Great Wall you see in photos, rebuilt by the Ming.
explain: Japan chose another path and from the 1630s shut its doors to most foreigners, letting only Dutch and Chinese traders in at Nagasaki.
fact: In 1337 much of north India was ruled by the Delhi Sultanate, a Muslim state founded by Turkic rulers.
fact: In south India, the Hindu Vijayanagara Empire was founded in 1336 and became very rich from trade and farming; its capital near Hampi was one of the largest cities in the world around 1500.
fact: Babur, a descendant of Timur and of Genghis Khan, founded the Mughal Empire by defeating the Delhi Sultan at Panipat in 1526.
fact: The Mughal emperor Akbar, who ruled from 1556 to 1605, expanded the empire and welcomed people of different faiths to his court.
fact: Under the Mughals India was one of the richest regions in the world, famous for its cotton cloth.
fact: The Taj Mahal in Agra was built by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, mostly between 1632 and 1648, by about 20,000 workers.
fact: In 1337 China was ruled by the Mongol Yuan dynasty, which the Chinese Ming dynasty overthrew in 1368.
fact: Most of the Great Wall you see in photos today was built or rebuilt by the Ming dynasty.
fact: The Great Wall cannot be seen with the naked eye from space, because although it is very long it is narrow.
fact: The Forbidden City in Beijing was the palace of Chinese emperors from 1420 until 1912, and it is now a museum.
fact: The Qing dynasty, founded by the Manchus from the north-east, took Beijing in 1644 and ruled China until 1912 as its last dynasty of emperors, and under the Qing China reached its largest size.
fact: From the 1630s Japan's Tokugawa rulers strictly limited contact with foreigners, allowing only Dutch and Chinese traders at Nagasaki, until the 1850s.
fact: The Safavid dynasty took power in Persia in 1501 and made Shia Islam the state religion, while their Ottoman neighbours and rivals were Sunni Muslims, and its capital Isfahan was one of the finest cities of the 1600s.
exercise: Who ruled much of north India in 1337? | The Delhi Sultanate | The Mughal Empire | The British East India Company | The Ming dynasty
exercise: Which great empire ruled south India in the 1400s and 1500s? | Vijayanagara | The Safavids | The Qing | The Yuan
exercise: Who founded the Mughal Empire? | Babur | Akbar the Great | Shah Jahan | Genghis Khan
exercise: Where did Babur defeat the Delhi Sultan in 1526? | Panipat | Agra Fort | Hampi in the south | Isfahan in Persia
exercise: Why is Akbar remembered? | He expanded the empire and welcomed people of different faiths | He built the Great Wall of China to keep out the Mughals | He closed India to all foreigners and their ships for ever | He founded the Ming dynasty
exercise: Why was the Taj Mahal built? | As a tomb for Shah Jahan's wife Mumtaz Mahal | As a strong fort to keep the British out of Agra | As a palace for Akbar's army | As a temple for the Ming emperors
exercise: Which dynasty ruled China in 1337? | The Mongol Yuan | The Ming | The Qing | The Mughals of Delhi
exercise: When did the Ming overthrow the Yuan? | 1368 | 1644 | 1912 | 1526
exercise: Who built or rebuilt most of the Great Wall seen in photos today? | The Ming dynasty | The Mongol Yuan | The Mughal emperors | The British
exercise: Can you see the Great Wall with the naked eye from space? | No, it is long but too narrow | Yes, it is the only building you can see | Yes, but only at night | No, because it is underground
exercise: What was the Forbidden City? | The palace of Chinese emperors in Beijing | A walled city where trade was banned | A prison for foreign merchants in Canton | A Mughal fort in Delhi
exercise: Who were the Manchus? | A people from the north-east who founded the Qing | Traders from Nagasaki | Soldiers of the Delhi Sultanate who invaded China | The rulers of Persia
exercise: When did the Qing take Beijing? | 1644 | 1368 | 1420 | 1501
exercise: Which Europeans were still allowed to trade with Japan after the 1630s? | The Dutch | The Portuguese | The Spanish | The English
exercise: Which dynasty made Shia Islam the state religion of Persia in 1501? | The Safavids | The Mughal emperors | The Ottoman sultans | The Qing emperors
exercise: Which city was the Safavids' great capital in the 1600s? | Isfahan | Agra in India | Beijing in China | Delhi on the Yamuna
test: lol my cousin says the Great Wall is the only thing you can see from space. true? | No, it's too narrow to see with the naked eye | Yes, astronauts see it every day from their windows | Yes, but only from the Moon | No, because it was knocked down
test: You start EU5 in 1337 and look for the Mughals in India. Why can't you find them? | They were founded only in 1526 | They had already fallen by 1337 | They lived in Persia then | They were a Chinese dynasty
test: A friend says the Taj Mahal is a palace where the emperor lived. What is wrong? | It is a tomb Shah Jahan built for his wife | Nothing, it was Akbar's bedroom and throne room | It is a church built by the British | It is a Ming fort
test: If you visited Beijing in 1500, who would be living in the Forbidden City? | The Ming emperor | The Mughal emperor | The Qing emperor | Nobody, it was built in 1912
test: A Portuguese ship sails into Nagasaki in 1700. What is likely to happen? | It is turned away, since only Dutch and Chinese may trade | It is welcomed as the only foreign trader allowed in all of Japan | It is given the city | Nothing, Japan had no rules
test: Which happened first? | The Ming took power from the Yuan | The Qing took Beijing | The Mughal Empire was founded | The Taj Mahal was finished
test: bruh why was India so rich back then | Trade, farming and famous cotton cloth | It had nearly all the world's gold mines | It got loads of money from Britain | It sold oil to Europe
test: A traveller in 1500 visits a huge Hindu capital near Hampi in south India. Whose capital is it? | Vijayanagara's | The Delhi Sultanate's | The Mughals' | The Safavids'
test: Why did the Safavids and the Ottomans often fight? | They were rival neighbours, and the Safavids were Shia, the Ottomans Sunni | Both wanted to rule Japan | The Safavids wanted to take the Taj Mahal from the Mughals | They fought over the Great Wall
test: My brother says the Qing were Chinese from the south. Right? | No, they were Manchus from the north-east | Yes, they came from the far south, near Hong Kong | No, they were Mongols from Persia | Yes, they were Ming princes
test: When did China's last imperial dynasty fall? | 1912 | 1644 | 1368 | 1850
exam: Identify the dynasty which exercised sovereignty over China in 1337. | The Mongol Yuan dynasty | The Ming dynasty | The Qing dynasty | The Mughal dynasty
exam: Ascertain the circumstances under which the Mughal Empire was established. | Babur defeated the Sultan of Delhi at Panipat in 1526 | Akbar inherited it from the Ming in 1556 | Shah Jahan purchased it from the Kingdom of Portugal in 1632 | The Safavids founded it in 1501
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the visibility of the Great Wall from space? | It is not discernible to the naked eye on account of its narrowness | It is the sole artificial structure visible from the surface of the Moon | It is visible solely during the hours of darkness | It is plainly visible from any orbit
exam: State the policy pursued by the Tokugawa rulers of Japan from the 1630s with regard to foreign persons. | Contact was strictly limited, with only Dutch and Chinese traders admitted at Nagasaki | All foreign merchants were welcomed without restriction | Only Portuguese traders were permitted to remain | Japan was ceded to the Dutch East India Company
